# FlowEnergetic / EXPLOSIVE-FILLED COPPER VESSEL, booster ignited, 2D PLANAR  (Hydro2, energetic.on = 1, elastic.on = 1)
#
# After test 4.3 of Wallis, Barton & Nikiforakis, Comput. Struct. 254 (2021) 106578 (the copper can of Schoch,
# Nordin-Bates & Nikiforakis, JCP 252 (2013) 163), booster-ignited version (no partition): a copper can, 24.6 cm
# long, wall 2 cm, inner half-height 9.6 cm, filled with the idealised explosive and surrounded by air; a 1 cm
# booster at 1e9 Pa at the left end of the charge starts the detonation.  Domain 40 cm x 20 cm, 800 x 400 cells
# (0.5 mm, the paper's resolution), symmetry plane on y = 0.  Copper: ideal plasticity, yield stress 0.07 GPa.
# DIFFERENCES FROM THE PAPER:
#   * PLANAR, not cylindrically symmetric (Hydro2 has no axisymmetric source terms): this is a slab can, the
#     divergence of the flow is different and the times of the paper's Fig. 4 do not carry over.
#   * the can dimensions are read off the paper's Fig. 4 (24.6 cm long, 2 cm walls, 9.6 cm inner radius).
#   * explosive, copper and mixture closure as in input_1D_DetonationCopper.
# SETTINGS THAT MATTER (changelog II.60):
#   * 0.01 % of gas in the metal and in the charge (eta = 1 - 1e-4), as Favrie & Schmidmayer 2026 carry in their solids:
#     with 1e-6 the trace is squeezed below `cutoff` when the detonation loads the wall and the cell collapses.
#   * the copper | explosive boundary starts SHARP (w = 1e-6 m) and species.order = 3 (THINC, Wallis 2021 App. A) keeps
#     it so: a layer of cells holding copper and reactant together ignites ahead of the detonation (a one-cell
#     tanh layer gives a front running along the wall at ~24 km/s).
#   * 0.5 mm cells are the paper's resolution, but at 0.5 mm the detonation of this explosive runs ~15 % fast here
#     (1D: +15 % at 0.5 mm, -0.06 % at 0.25 mm, -0.6 % at 0.1 mm); use amr.n_cell = 1600 800 for the wave speed.
# Plots:  python3 tests/FlowEnergetic/reference/copper_can_plots.py <run_dir>
